---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const people = await getCollection('people');
const docs = await getCollection('documents');
const arts = await getCollection('artifacts');
const places = await getCollection('places');

// A small hand-picked strip of photographs for the landing page — chosen to
// span the lines and the eras (1899 → 1980s) and to put faces on the door.
const featuredIds = [
  'eesley-family-group-portrait-1899',
  'charlie-eesley-july-1970-vietnam',
  'eesley-wedding-1971-portrait',
  'wildermuth-family-1968-itazuke',
  'black-lake-family-on-dock-c1950s',
  'terrie-reading-to-chuck-c1984',
];
const byId = new Map(arts.map((a) => [a.id, a]));
const featured = featuredIds
  .map((id) => byId.get(id))
  .filter((a): a is NonNullable<typeof a> => Boolean(a));

// The "Enter" cards — the real navigation into the archive.
const entries = [
  {
    href: '/docs/family-threads/',
    title: 'Family threads',
    note: 'the synthesis essay — nine cross-generational values, bilingual',
    star: true,
  },
  { href: '/family/', title: 'The family', note: `${people.length} people` },
  { href: '/docs/', title: 'Stories & memoirs', note: `${docs.length} documents` },
  {
    href: '/places/',
    title: 'Places, then & now',
    note: `${places.length} sites — including the German villages, photographed across generations`,
  },
  { href: '/archive/', title: 'Photo & letter archive', note: `${arts.length} artifacts` },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Eesley · Wildermuth" description="A narrative archive of the Eesley and Wildermuth families.">
  <section class="max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-serif mb-2">Eesley · Wildermuth</h1>
    <p class="text-ink/70 italic mt-0 mb-8">
      A narrative archive of two American families &mdash; one out of Stratford-on-Avon
      by way of a Scottish flour mill, one out of Württemberg by way of Marietta &mdash;
      meeting in a single generation.
    </p>
  </section>

  {featured.length > 0 && (
    <section class="not-prose mb-10">
      <ul class="grid grid-cols-3 sm:grid-cols-6 gap-2 sm:gap-3">
        {featured.map((a) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/archive/${a.id}/`} class="block no-underline group" title={a.data.title}>
              <div class="aspect-square overflow-hidden bg-rule/40 border border-rule">
                <Image
                  src={a.data.image}
                  alt={a.data.title}
                  widths={[150, 300]}
                  sizes="(max-width: 640px) 33vw, 150px"
                  class="w-full h-full object-cover transition group-hover:opacity-90"
                />
              </div>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="prose max-w-prose">
    <p class="mt-0">
      This site was inspired by the work of <strong>Robert Earl Wildermuth</strong>, Chuck's maternal
      grandfather, who in his retirement turned to genealogy and wrote the memoirs, the
      combat log, the &ldquo;Return to Germany&rdquo; travelogue, and the German-ancestor sketches
      that frame everything maternal here. He closed his last manuscript by addressing
      <em>&ldquo;future genealogists.&rdquo;</em> This site is one of them answering. Their work is
      the archive; this site is the next layer of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* Enter — the real way in, surfaced high on the page. */}
  <section class="not-prose my-10">
    <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ink/50 mb-4">Enter</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
      {entries.map((e) => (
        <li>
          <a
            href={e.href}
            class="block h-full no-underline border border-rule rounded-sm px-4 py-3 transition hover:border-sepia hover:bg-sepia/5"
          >
            <span class="font-serif text-lg">
              {e.star && <span class="text-sepia" aria-hidden="true">★ </span>}{e.title}
            </span>
            <span class="block text-sm text-ink/60 mt-1">{e.note}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose max-w-prose">
    <p>
      <strong>The Zhou and Li side &mdash; live and growing, in two languages.</strong>
      The Eesley and Wildermuth lines have a year of intensive work behind them; the
      <strong>Zhou and Li families of Qingdao, Shandong</strong> &mdash;
      <a href="/family/lijie-zhou/">Lijie</a>'s line, both her father's Zhou family and her
      mother's Li and Shang families &mdash; is built out four generations deep
      and being extended branch by branch. <strong>Every Zhou- and Li-side page carries both English
      and <span lang="zh">中文</span> versions on the same page</strong> &mdash;
      paragraphs in English at the top, then a parallel <span lang="zh">中文</span> section below.
      Open the <a href="/zhou-li/">English guide to Lijie's family</a> to navigate that side in English,
      or the dedicated <a href="/zh/" lang="zh">中文 hub</a> for the Chinese-first entry point &mdash;
      or read any Zhou- or Li-side person, place, or story page in either language. Name characters and
      earlier generations are still being confirmed with the family.
    </p>

    <hr class="my-8 border-rule" />

    <h2 class="text-2xl mt-8">The keepers of this archive</h2>
    <p>
      Four keepers built the paternal side. <strong><a href="/family/mary-eesley-bean/">Mary Eesley Bean</a></strong>'s 1985 indented register laid
      down the relationships from Stratford through to Marietta. <strong><a href="/family/margaret-maggie-eesley/">Margaret &ldquo;Maggie&rdquo; Eesley</a></strong>'s
      four-generation photo archive carries the faces. <strong><a href="/family/jeanne-eesley-kamiab/">Aunt Jeanne (Eesley) Kamiab</a></strong>
      contributed many of the photographs and the oral history this site relies on &mdash;
      including the wartime account of how Stella Sunn came to live with the Eesleys.
      <strong><a href="/family/roberta-burnes/">Roberta Burnes</a></strong> &mdash; Helen Burnes&apos;s daughter, Chuck&apos;s
      first cousin once removed &mdash; has carried forward much of the Charles Leonard
      Eesley material into the next generation, including the labeled c. 1973&ndash;1975
      Charles Leonard reunion portrait at Highland Ridge, the family-label transcription
      that resolved the c. 1948&ndash;1949 Charles Leonard group portrait, the 2019
      family-email reconstruction that placed Stella Sunn&apos;s arrival within months of
      Uncle Lyle&apos;s Cabanatuan death, and the upstream provenance of the deepest-ancestor
      portraits of Joseph Hill Chenoweth and John K. Timmons (the family album she
      inherited via the Burnes&ndash;Eesley line, which reached this site via Maggie&apos;s deck).
      She also keeps the <a href="/docs/charles-leonard-letter-collection/">1940s Eesley letter collection</a>
      &mdash; a large body of Charles Leonard&apos;s wartime correspondence in his own
      hand, almost indecipherable, not yet read or digitized; the single most
      consequential undeciphered source still to come.
    </p>

    <p>
      A fifth keeper, still at work, is <strong><a href="/family/dale-eesley-genealogist/">Dale Eesley</a></strong>, whose careful and thorough
      genealogical work on <a href="https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/GMLW-PXW">FamilySearch</a>
      is the most current and best-sourced single account of the line. Chuck collaborates
      with Dale on the same FamilySearch tree; a parallel Ancestry.com version of the tree
      exists as well, but Dale and Chuck treat the FamilySearch one as canonical. The
      <a href="/docs/dale-eesley-familysearch-tree/">structured tree as a downloadable GEDCOM</a>
      &mdash; 3,428 individuals across 1,510 families &mdash; is here too. It is the source future
      Eesley genealogists should consult first.
    </p>

    <hr class="my-8 border-rule" />

    <h2 class="text-2xl mt-8">On living people</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80">
      <strong>Living people get a light touch.</strong> Specific birth dates, exact
      home locations, and minor children's names are not surfaced for anyone who
      hasn't explicitly opted in. The address appendix from
      <a href="/docs/eesley-family-history-1985/">Mary Bean's 1985 register</a> is
      held offline. The fuller editorial note is on the
      <a href="/about/privacy/">archive's privacy page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
